{"person_id":"pieter-wuille","name":"Pieter Wuille","cohort_group":"Researchers / independent engineers","category":"Bitcoin Core developer / cryptographer","birth_year":1988,"age_at_milestone":24,"milestone_by_age_26":"Became a Bitcoin Core maintainer at age 22 in May 2011, authored BIP-32 (Hierarchical Deterministic Wallets) at age 24 in 2012, created libsecp256k1 at age 25 in 2013, and co-founded Blockstream at age 26 in 2014.","early_history_summary":"Pieter Wuille learned to code in BASIC at age 11 and C at age 16-17, then graduated with great distinction from KU Leuven with a master's in Computer Science Engineering in 2007 at age 19. He discovered Bitcoin through the Freenode IRC network in late 2010 while completing his PhD at KU Leuven/Ghent University. He made his first contribution to Bitcoin Core in March 2011 and was granted commit access just six weeks later by Gavin Andresen, making him the second long-term Bitcoin Core maintainer. He authored BIP-32 in 2012 and started libsecp256k1 in 2013, both foundational to Bitcoin's infrastructure.","primary_leverage_engine":"Technical depth","normalized_primary_engine":"Scarce technical / intellectual depth","secondary_engine":"Early specialization","started_serious_reps_before_20_score":1,"prior_reps_score":2,"scarce_skill_depth_score":3,"native_distribution_score":0,"elite_ecosystem_network_score":2,"complementary_team_score":1,"structural_wave_score":3,"concentration_intensity_score":2,"capital_safety_score":0,"domain_proximity_score":1,"total_leverage_score":15,"success_tier":3,"leverage_evidence_confidence":"Medium","family_context_summary":"Not documented in reviewed sources.","parent_family_domain_summary":"Not documented in reviewed sources.","early_family_financial_platform_support_score":0,"parent_family_domain_advantage_score":0,"inherited_audience_business_network_score":0,"elite_institution_performance_pipeline_score":1,"frontier_geography_ecosystem_score":0,"rare_early_tools_facilities_score":0,"dedicated_mentor_coach_tutor_score":1,"exceptional_peer_cofounder_sibling_score":1,"early_online_platform_community_score":2,"direct_customer_domain_exposure_score":1,"prodigy_physical_edge_score":1,"adversity_constraint_catalyst_score":0,"inherited_access_stack_count":6,"exceptional_inherited_access_count":1,"all_documented_early_condition_count":6,"primary_early_advantage_archetype":"Platform-native compounding","primary_early_advantage_tags":"online community, IRC, Bitcoin forum, technical depth, early coding, PhD, peer network","evidence_summary":"Pieter Wuille learned to code at age 11 and completed a PhD in Computer Science at KU Leuven by age 23. He discovered Bitcoin through the Freenode IRC network in late 2010 and made his first contribution to Bitcoin Core in March 2011. Within six weeks, Gavin Andresen granted him commit access, making him the second long-term Bitcoin Core maintainer. He authored BIP-32 (Hierarchical Deterministic Wallets) in 2012, which became the foundation for every modern Bitcoin wallet, and started libsecp256k1 in 2013, a high-performance cryptographic library that replaced OpenSSL in Bitcoin Core. He co-founded Blockstream in 2014. His family background is not documented in reviewed sources.","early_advantage_evidence_confidence":"Medium","source_count":5,"primary_source_url":"https://bitcoin-institute.pages.dev/participants/pieter-wuille/","source_urls":["https://bitcoin-institute.pages.dev/participants/pieter-wuille/","https://hrf.org/program/financial-freedom/finney-prize/about-peter-wuille-and-gregory-maxwell/","https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0032.mediawiki","https://wuille.net/work/","https://bitcoindefense.org/assets/documents/Pieter-Wuilles-First-Witness-Statement99454993.1.pdf"],"annotation_status":"subagent_researched_beta","source_audit_status":"not_independently_audited","data_version":"0.3.0-trajectory-beta","eligibility_status":"age_26_eligible","starting_point":"Not documented in reviewed sources.","current_position":"Engineer at Chaincode Labs in New York City; co-founded Blockstream in 2014; continues to author Bitcoin Improvement Proposals and core code as of 2025.","current_position_year":2025,"is_living":true,"trajectory":[{"year":2007,"age":19,"event":"Graduated with great distinction from KU Leuven with a master's degree in Engineering, specialization Computer Science."},{"year":2011,"age":23,"event":"Obtained PhD in Computer Science from KU Leuven/Ghent University; made first contribution to Bitcoin Core (PR #122) on March 17 and was granted commit access on May 1, becoming the second long-term Bitcoin Core maintainer."},{"year":2012,"age":24,"event":"Authored BIP-32 (Hierarchical Deterministic Wallets), assigned February 11, 2012, which became the foundation for every modern Bitcoin wallet key derivation system."},{"year":2013,"age":25,"event":"Started libsecp256k1 on March 5, a high-performance C library for elliptic curve cryptography that later replaced OpenSSL as Bitcoin Core's signature backend."},{"year":2014,"age":26,"event":"Co-founded Blockstream, a leading Bitcoin technology company, with Adam Back, Gregory Maxwell, and other Bitcoin Core developers."},{"year":2017,"age":29,"event":"Co-authored BIP-141 (Segregated Witness), a major Bitcoin protocol upgrade that fixed transaction malleability and enabled the Lightning Network."},{"year":2021,"age":33,"event":"Co-authored BIP-340, BIP-341, and BIP-342 (Schnorr signatures and Taproot), enhancing Bitcoin's privacy, efficiency, and script flexibility."},{"year":2022,"age":34,"event":"Stepped down from Bitcoin Core commit access after 11 years as maintainer, having contributed tens of thousands of lines of code to the codebase."}]}
